 "grammar": {
  "title": "Past Continuous and Past Simple",
  "explanation": "Use the **past simple** for a short finished action in the past. Use the **past continuous** for a longer action that was in progress.\n\nThe past continuous form is **was** or **were** plus a verb with **-ing**. I was, you were, he was, she was, they were.\n\nWe often use them together in one sentence. The long action goes in the **past continuous**, and the short action goes in the **past simple**.\n\nLook for time words. Words like **while**, **when**, and **at nine o'clock** tell you which form to use.",
  "examples": [
   "I *was sleeping* at nine o'clock. (an action in progress at a past time)",
   "She *was cooking* dinner when I arrived. (a long action plus a short action)",
   "The eleven players *were running* on the field at seven. (they were in the middle of running)",
   "I *broke* my pen while I *was writing*. (short finished action plus long action)",
   "They *were watching* TV when the phone *rang*. (long action stopped by a short action)",
   "We *were talking* about the twelve months of the year. (an action in progress in the past)"
  ],
